# Satisfiability and realizability of temporal formulas.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
## Formulas using until/release/weak_until/since/trigger or nested liveness
## (G (F ...)) are decided by calling ltlsynt (from Spot). Without ltlsynt on
## the PATH those verdicts come back UNKNOWN.

# A temporal formula describes a reactive system: at each time point the
# environment picks the input streams i1, i2, ..., and the system answers
# with the output streams o1, o2, ...

# Two pipelines decide temporal formulas. Boolean combinations of top-level
# always/sometimes statements over non-temporal bodies are the safety
# fragment, decided by Tau's own safety pipeline; the binary operators
# until/release/weak_until/since/trigger and nested liveness such as
# G (F phi) are decided by reactive synthesis through ltlsynt.

# In the safety fragment sat already quantifies over the inputs: the
# specification must hold whatever they are. The first formula is
# satisfiable; the second is not, since the input can stay 0:

sat F (o1[t] = 1)
sat F (i1[t] = 1)

# Realizability is the reactive question: is there a strategy for the
# outputs that keeps the formula true against EVERY input behaviour? The
# nested forms below route through synthesis, and there who controls the
# stream decides the verdict. The system can grant o1 infinitely often:

realizable G (F (o1[t] = 1))

# But no output strategy can force the environment to send 1s, so the same
# shape over an input is unrealizable:

unrealizable G (F (i1[t] = 1))

# valid holds when the formula cannot be escaped at all:

valid G (o1[t] | o1[t]' = 1)

# What follows are classic synthesis benchmarks, each answered in
# milliseconds.

# Request/grant: every request is eventually granted:

realizable G ((i1[t] = 1) -> F ((o1[t] = 1)))

# Echo: the output mirrors the input at every step:

realizable G ((o1[t] = 1) <-> (i1[t] = 1))

# Response with delay: what came in at t-1 constrains the output at t:

realizable G ((i1[t-1] = 1) -> (o1[t] = 1))

# An impossibility: always 1 and eventually 0 cannot both hold:

realizable (G ((o1[t] = 1))) && (F ((o1[t] = 0)))

# Mutual exclusion:

realizable G (!((o1[t] = 1) && (o2[t] = 1)))

# A two-client arbiter: every request is eventually granted and the grants
# never overlap:

realizable (G ((i1[t] = 1) -> F ((o1[t] = 1)))) && (G ((i2[t] = 1) -> F ((o2[t] = 1)))) && (G (!((o1[t] = 1) && (o2[t] = 1))))

# Weak until and persistence:

realizable ((o1[t] = 1) weak_until (i1[t] = 1))
realizable F (G ((o1[t] = 1)))

# First-order quantifiers compose with the temporal operators when they sit
# inside them:

realizable G (F (ex x o3[t]:bv[8] = x))

# A quantifier over streams with a temporal operator under it has no
# proposition in the skeleton, so this one answers UNKNOWN (see Known LTL
# limitations):

realizable G (all x ex y F ((o3[t]:bv[8] = x:bv[8]) && (o4[t]:bv[8] = y:bv[8])))

# The ltl command shows the whole decision pipeline for one formula: the
# data atoms it abstracts, the propositional LTL skeleton handed to
# ltlsynt, the strategy automaton that comes back, the ABA oracle checks on
# its transitions, how run executes the formula, and the verdict:

ltl G (F (o1[t] = 0))

# ltl runs the same pipeline on a top-level F, and the data game gives its
# strategy:

ltl F (o1[t] = 1)

# An assume-guarantee specification is an implication decided by the game. The
# arbiter is realizable under its assumption that the requests never coincide:

realizable ((G !(i5[t]:bv[1] = 1 && i6[t]:bv[1] = 1))) -> ((G (i5[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (i6[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 0)))

# Without the assumption the environment raises both at once, and
# unrealizable_core names the conjuncts that conflict:

realizable (G (i5[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (i6[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 0))
unrealizable_core (G (i5[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 1)) && (G (i6[t]:bv[1] = 1 -> o5[t]:bv[1] = 0))

# explain prints the same core, and the environment's counter-strategy when
# ltlsynt decided the core. The environment wins this until by never raising
# its input:

explain (o8[t]:bv[1] = 1) U (i8[t]:bv[1] = 1)

# ltl-semantics finite reads a formula over finite traces. Two values that recur
# forever are satisfiable on an infinite trace, and on a finite one the last
# step holds only one of them:

sat (G(F(o7[t]:bv[1] = 0))) && (G(F(o7[t]:bv[1] = 1)))
set ltl-semantics finite
sat (G(F(o7[t]:bv[1] = 0))) && (G(F(o7[t]:bv[1] = 1)))
set ltl-semantics infinite

quit
